<!-- Response 2.1:

Your overall picture is right. A few details are off, and some of them matter for the policies and the README.

What you've got right
main.tf defines the compute and storage, and network.tf the networking. ✅
aws_instance.web gets t3.micro from variables.tf. ✅
root_block_device is encrypted and 8 GiB. ✅ (More precisely, it's the boot disk the operating system lives on, rather than storage for the instance to "exist in".)
The VPC gets 10.0.0.0/16 from variables.tf. ✅
SSH is only allowed from 203.0.113.0/24. ✅ (That's a reserved documentation range, i.e. a placeholder for "your office/VPN".)
Corrections
1. File names don't affect what a file can contain.
Terraform loads every .tf file in the folder as one configuration. Splitting into main.tf / network.tf is purely a convention for humans. The VPC is just as much a "required AWS resource" as the instance, and you could put everything in one file and it would work identically.

2. Region vs availability zone, and how each is set.

The EBS volume doesn't set a region itself. The region comes from the provider (region = var.aws_region in providers.tf) and applies to every resource.
What the volume does set is an availability zone, copied from the subnet: eu-west-1a.
A region (eu-west-1, Dublin) contains several AZs (eu-west-1a, -1b, -1c), which are separate data-centre groups. The subnet is in AZ eu-west-1a, not "the eu-west-1 availability zone".
Small but important for code: it's eu-west-1 with hyphens. eu_west_1 would fail validation.
3. "Under EU digital jurisdiction" needs a caveat, and it's a key README point.
Data stored in Dublin is subject to Irish/EU law, but AWS is a US company, so it's also subject to US laws such as the CLOUD Act, which can compel US providers to hand over data regardless of where it's stored. That's exactly the "limitations of region-based data residency controls" the brief asks you to discuss: location ≠ jurisdiction. Customer-managed encryption keys and EU-owned "sovereign cloud" offerings exist largely because of this.

4. map_public_ip_on_launch = false doesn't block public connections by itself.
It means instances launched in the subnet don't automatically get a public IP address. Without one, nothing on the internet can address them. But:

You could still attach a public IP manually (e.g. an Elastic IP).
More importantly, this VPC has no internet gateway at all, so there's no route to or from the internet anyway. The stack is currently completely private, and that's actually what isolates it.
5. The security group doesn't open up the VPC.
This is the main misunderstanding. A security group is a firewall attached to individual instances (strictly, to their network interfaces), not to the VPC. It only applies to resources that list it, here only aws_instance.web via vpc_security_group_ids. So it means "the web server accepts HTTPS from anywhere", not "the VPC allows full internet access".

Also, from_port = 443, to_port = 443 isn't "to and from"; it's a port range (start to end). It's just a range of one port. from_port = 8000, to_port = 8100 would open 101 ports.

In practice, because of point 4, that 443 rule can't receive anything from the internet right now. It's written for when you add an internet gateway and a public IP.

6. Egress: 0.0.0.0/0 means "any address", not "default gateway".

0.0.0.0/0 is the CIDR for every IPv4 address. It looks like the default route in a route table, which is why it's easy to mix up, but here it just means "any destination".
Egress controls connections the instance starts, e.g. downloading updates, not replies.
Replies to inbound connections are allowed automatically, because security groups are stateful: if a connection was allowed in, its response is always allowed out, whatever the egress rules say. You could delete the egress block entirely and HTTPS replies would still work. -->

